.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt NVIDIA-UVM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvidia-uvm
.Nd NVIDIA unified virtual memory driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "kldload nvidia-uvm"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
module provides the two device nodes the NVIDIA CUDA user-mode driver
.Pq libcuda
needs beside
.Pa /dev/nvidiactl :
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/nvidia-uvm-tools"
.It Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm
The main device.
One open file is one UVM virtual address space, bound to the address
space of the process that initialised it.
All GPU registrations, mappings and memory requests go here.
.It Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm-tools
The profiler and debugger event interface.
.El
.Pp
Both nodes are created mode 0666 when the module loads, as libcuda runs as
an ordinary user.
The module is a client of the resource manager inside the NVIDIA GPU
driver and needs an
.Pa nvidia.ko
that exports the UVM kernel interface.
.Pp
CUDA programs run under the Linuxulator; the module registers a
.Xr linux 4
ioctl handler so the request numbers libcuda issues reach it unchanged.
A native
.Fx
program wraps the same numbers with the encoding in
.In nvidia_uvm_ioctl.h .
.Pp
This is a clean-room implementation written from a functional
specification (revision r1), not from NVIDIA's Linux driver.
The present phase drives Blackwell GB20x GPUs
.Pq GeForce RTX 50 series
and covers GPU, GPU address space and channel registration, external
allocations
.Pq Fn cudaMalloc , Fn cudaHostAlloc ,
semaphore pools and dynamic-parallelism regions, with every GPU fault
cancelled.
Managed memory
.Pq Fn cudaMallocManaged ,
peer access, access counters and the tools event queues are later phases;
their requests answer
.Dv NV_ERR_INVALID_ADDRESS
or
.Dv NV_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED
as the specification prescribes and log the phase at most once
every ten seconds.
The same limit applies to the message for a failed GPU lookup by UUID and
to the logged
.Va rmStatus
of a failing request, so a client that retries in a loop cannot fill the
kernel message buffer.
Other GPU generations report themselves unsupported at registration.
.Pp
The Linuxulator runs a registered ioctl handler with Giant held.
The driver releases Giant for the duration of each Linux ioctl and relies on
it nowhere: its locks are
.Xr sx 9
and
.Xr mutex 9
locks of its own, and its task queues are not bound to Giant.
Giant users such as USB enumeration therefore do not wait behind a long
GPU mapping.
The loops that build page tables yield the CPU between tables, and a
mapping request stops with
.Dv NV_ERR_SIGNAL_PENDING
when the calling thread has a signal pending, after releasing the page
tables it had taken.
Unmapping does not stop early.
Page tables and the probe page are single-page DMA allocations; the
GPU DMA tag is one page in size, as
.Xr bus_dma 9
allocates the whole tag size per allocation.
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.builtin_tests
Built-in tests enabled; default 0, when unknown request numbers fail with
.Er ENOSYS .
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.enable_va_space_mm
Bind each VA space to the creating process address space; default 1.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.gpfifo_entries
GPFIFO entries per UVM channel, a power of two in [32, 1048576]; default 1024.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.fault_batch_count
Replayable faults fetched per batch; default 256.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.wait_timeout_ms
How long a wait on GPU work may take before the module records a fatal
error; default 30000.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.sysmem_gpfifo
Place the GPFIFO ring and PUT word in system memory (1, the default) or
video memory (0).
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.debug_push
Log the method stream, GPFIFO entry, GP_PUT and doorbell value of the first
N pushes (at most 64), and the GPU addresses of each channel's GPFIFO,
GP_PUT, doorbell, pushbuffer and tracking semaphore; default 0 (off).
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.init_probe
Run channel initialisation in stages, each followed by a host-class semaphore
release and a log line, with the copy engine first used in the last stage;
default 0.
Diagnostic for a copy-engine error during the first push.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.init_probe_work
With
.Va init_probe ,
a bit mask of first-work stages run on channel 0 after the initialisation
stages, each a push followed by a wait and a check of the written data:
1 virtual copy, 2 virtual memset, 4 physical system-memory copy, 8 physical
memset without flush, 16 the same with flush, 32 plus a host wait for idle,
64 plus a host membar (the push that raised Xid 71), 128 as 32 completed by a
host-class semaphore release;
256 a copy-engine semaphore release into the semaphore page, 512 a virtual copy
page to page, 1024 the same with LINE_COUNT 1, 2048 a memset with source pitch,
explicit remap and LINE_COUNT, 4096 a memset with the default words, 8192 as
2048 with a block-linear source (raises Xid 71);
16384 to 2097152 (bits 14 to 21) a second series on copy-engine write
visibility: host semaphore control, copy-engine semaphore with a copy-engine
or host marker, copy, memset, memset with host WFI and MEMBAR, and physical
memsets of a probe page.
Default 0.
Diagnostic.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.ce_bind_subch4
Also bind the copy class on subchannel 4 during channel initialisation;
default 0.
Diagnostic, see spec question Q-008.
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.fatal_error
The module-wide fatal status; 0 until an unrecoverable error (a channel
error, a timeout, a lost GPU) is recorded, after which every request fails
with it until the module is reloaded.
.It Va hw.nvidia_uvm.gpus
One line per registered GPU with its architecture, classes and fault
statistics.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/nvidia-uvm-tools" -compact
.It Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm
.It Pa /dev/nvidia-uvm-tools
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr linux 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in CloudBSD in 2026.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An REVYTECH Inc
.Sh BUGS
Only Blackwell GB20x has a hardware abstraction layer in this phase;
Ampere, Ada, Hopper and Blackwell GB100 GPUs are refused at registration.
The semaphore pool mapping cannot verify that the file offset equals the
mapped address, as the specification asks: the
.Fx
device mmap path does not pass the address to the driver.
